# Trivy ignore list.
#
# RULES FOR THIS FILE — the same discipline as config/owasp/suppressions.xml:
#
# 1. An entry states that a finding does NOT apply to this project, or that it
#    applies and has no available remedy. It is never a way to make a red build
#    green. If a fix exists, apply the fix.
#      Worked example: CVE-2026-54291 (SCRAM-SHA-256 MITM bypass in the
#      PostgreSQL JDBC driver) is NOT in this file — Trivy reported
#      "fixed in: 42.7.12", so the driver was pinned to 42.7.13 in pom.xml
#      instead. A published fix is always an upgrade, never an ignore.
#
# 2. Every entry carries the reason and a review date, no more than 90 days out.
#
# 3. Keep entries as NARROW as the syntax allows: a specific rule ID, never a
#    whole scanner or severity class. Never add `--severity` filtering to the
#    Trivy invocation to achieve the same effect silently — that hides future
#    findings too, and it hides them from everyone.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AWS-0104 — "A security group rule should not allow unrestricted egress to
#             any IP address."
#
# ACCEPTED, with the port range genuinely tightened.
#
# The egress rules in infra/ec2.tf were rewritten (attempt 9) from a single
# allow-everything rule (protocol "-1", all ports) to five explicit rules:
# 443/tcp, 80/tcp, 53/udp, 53/tcp and 123/udp. A compromised process can no
# longer open an outbound channel on a port of its choosing — which was the
# substantive risk.
#
# What remains is the DESTINATION being 0.0.0.0/0, and that is what AWS-0104
# actually checks (hence it now fires once per rule rather than once). The
# destination cannot be narrowed on this design:
#   - the host must reach Ubuntu archive mirrors, Maven Central and ghcr.io,
#     none of which publish stable IP ranges suitable for a security group;
#   - AWS publishes prefix lists for its OWN services (S3, CloudWatch), but not
#     for third-party package registries;
#   - the correct way to bound egress properly is a NAT gateway with a filtering
#     proxy, or VPC endpoints plus a private subnet. Both are Tier-2 network
#     changes with real cost, not a security-group edit.
#
# Compensating controls: the ports above are the complete set the host needs;
# the instance runs no user-facing shell; IMDSv2 is enforced so credentials
# cannot be scraped via SSRF; and outbound 25 (SMTP), 6667 (IRC) and every
# other port a commodity implant reaches for are closed.
#
# Review by: 2026-11-27. Revisit when/if this workload moves to a private
# subnet with a NAT gateway, at which point this entry should be DELETED.
AWS-0104

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AWS-0107 — "Security groups should not allow unrestricted ingress to SSH or
#             RDP from any IP address."
#
# ACCEPTED for the default configuration only, and parameterised so it can be
# closed without touching code.
#
# infra/ec2.tf now takes the SSH source from var.ssh_allowed_cidr. It defaults
# to 0.0.0.0/0 because the Ansible configure stage runs on GITHUB-HOSTED
# RUNNERS, whose egress addresses are dynamic and rotate frequently; pinning a
# CIDR would break every deploy without warning.
#
# TWO REAL REMEDIES, both documented on the rule in infra/ec2.tf:
#   1. Set ssh_allowed_cidr to your own egress range — correct for a
#      self-hosted runner or a bastion, and a one-line tfvars change.
#   2. Migrate the configure stage to AWS SSM Session Manager and delete the
#      ingress rule entirely. The IAM role and instance profile are already in
#      place and IMDSv2 is already enforced, so this is the recommended
#      production end state and removes port 22 from the internet completely.
#
# Compensating controls today: password authentication is disabled (key-only);
# the sole authorised key is the platform-managed deploy keypair, which is
# never written to the repository; and the application itself is not exposed —
# Spring Boot binds 127.0.0.1:8080 with nginx as the only public listener.
#
# Review by: 2026-11-27. DELETE this entry as soon as remedy 1 or 2 is adopted.
AWS-0107
